course: books
lesson: 45
title: The Wind in the Willows, chapter VI, part 1: Mr. Toad (the hour of Toad, and off with those motor-clothes!)
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: It is early summer. Rat and Mole have been up since dawn getting the boats ready — painting, varnishing, mending — when there is a heavy knock. It's the Badger! He never pays visits, so this is a big event. He stands there very serious and says: «The hour has come! … Toad's hour!» He is keeping his winter promise.
explain: He has heard that a new, extra-powerful motor-car is arriving at Toad Hall this very morning. The three march off in single file. In front of the Hall stands a huge shiny red car, and down the steps swaggers Toad in goggles, cap, gaiters, gauntlets and an enormous overcoat: «Come on, you fellows, for a jolly — a jolly — er — jolly…» His words fade when he sees their stern faces.
explain: The Badger tells the chauffeur he won't be wanted, and the friends bundle Toad inside. «Take those ridiculous things off!» «Shan't!» So the Rat sits on him and the Mole peels his motor-clothes off bit by bit. Without them Toad is just Toad again, and he giggles feebly.
explain: The Badger lays it out: Toad has ignored every warning, is wasting the money his father left him, and is giving all the animals a bad name with his crashes and quarrels with the police. «You will come with me into the smoking-room, and there you will hear some facts about yourself.» And he leads Toad in and shuts the door.
fact: The river «had resumed its wonted banks and its accustomed pace» (wonted = usual) — the winter flood has gone down. The sun seems to pull green things «up out of the earth towards him, as if by strings».
fact: Rat and Mole were «painting and varnishing, mending paddles, repairing cushions, hunting for missing boat-hooks» for «the opening of the boating season».
fact: «“Bother!” said the Rat, all over egg.» The Mole opens the door and announces «with much importance, “Mr. Badger!”»
fact: It is «a wonderful thing» that the Badger «should pay a formal call on them, or indeed on anybody»; usually you had to catch him along a hedgerow or hunt him up in his house in the Wood.
fact: «The hour has come!» — «What hour?» asks the Rat, glancing at the clock. — «Whose hour, you should rather say … Why, Toad's hour! … I said I would take him in hand as soon as the winter was well over».
fact: «Another new and exceptionally powerful motor-car will arrive at Toad Hall on approval or return» (on approval = to try out, and send back if you don't want it).
fact: The Badger mocks Toad's «singularly hideous habiliments» (very ugly clothes), which turn him into «an Object which throws any decent-minded animal … into a violent fit».
fact: The Rat: «We'll rescue the poor unhappy animal! We'll convert him! He'll be the most converted Toad that ever was». (Convert = change someone's mind completely.)
fact: «Animals when in company walk in a proper and sensible manner, in single file», so they can help each other «in case of sudden trouble or danger».
fact: The car is «of great size, painted a bright red (Toad's favourite colour)». Toad wears «goggles, cap, gaiters, and enormous overcoat», «drawing on his gauntleted gloves» (gaiters = leg coverings; gauntlets = long, wide-cuffed gloves).
fact: The Badger to the chauffeur (the paid driver): «I'm afraid you won't be wanted to-day … Mr. Toad has changed his mind … Please understand that this is final.»
fact: «Take those ridiculous things off!» — «Shan't!» (= shall not) «What is the meaning of this gross outrage? I demand an instant explanation.»
fact: «The Rat sat on him, and the Mole got his motor-clothes off him bit by bit». «Now that he was merely Toad, and no longer the Terror of the Highway, he giggled feebly». Much of his «blustering» (loud, bossy) spirit had gone with the clothes.
fact: The Badger: «you've gone on squandering the money your father left you (squander = waste), and you're getting us animals a bad name in the district by your furious driving and your smashes and your rows with the police».
fact: «Independence is all very well, but we animals never allow our friends to make fools of themselves beyond a certain limit; and that limit you've reached.» But: «I don't want to be too hard on you. I'll make one more effort to bring you to reason.»
fact: Talk in this lesson: «fixin' to» (southern US) = about to; «no cap» = honestly, no lie; «bussin'» = really good; «canny» (North East England) = quite, nice; «servo» (Australia) = petrol station.
exercise: What season is it at the start of chapter VI? | The early part of summer | Midwinter, with snow on the ground | Late autumn, with falling leaves | Christmas, with snow falling
exercise: What have Rat and Mole been doing since dawn? | Getting the boats ready for the boating season | Sleeping late after a party at Toad Hall | Digging a new tunnel to the Badger's | Fishing
exercise: «“Bother!” said the Rat, all over egg.» Why does he say «Bother»? | Someone knocks while he's eating breakfast | He has dropped his egg on the new boat cushions | He hates eggs | The Mole is late
exercise: Why is the Badger's visit «a wonderful thing»? | He hardly ever pays a call on anybody | He has never been to the river bank in his life before | He brings presents | He comes by boat
exercise: «The hour has come!» What does the Rat do? | Glances at the clock and asks «What hour?» | Jumps up shouting «Hooray!» and grabs his pistols | Falls asleep | Hides
exercise: Whose «hour» does the Badger mean? | Toad's | The weasels', who are coming to attack | The Mole's | Lunch time
exercise: Which promise is the Badger keeping? | To take Toad in hand once winter was over | To take Rat and Mole to see the old city again | To visit every Sunday | To teach Mole to row
exercise: What is arriving at Toad Hall that morning? | A new, very powerful motor-car | A new boat for the opening of the boating season | A caravan | A policeman
exercise: «On approval or return.» What does this mean? | To try out, and send back if not wanted | To buy at once and keep for ever | To borrow for one day | Stolen
exercise: How does the Badger describe Toad's motor-clothes? | Singularly hideous habiliments | Very fine and handsome clothes | Sensible clothes | Expensive but pretty
exercise: How do the three animals walk to Toad Hall? | In single file | Side by side across the road, arm in arm | Running as fast as they can | By boat along the river
exercise: Why do animals walk in single file, says the book? | To help each other in sudden trouble or danger | Because the roads are much too narrow for two | To hide | To look smart
exercise: What colour is the new car? | Bright red, Toad's favourite | Canary-yellow picked out with green | Black | Blue and white
exercise: Which of these is Toad NOT wearing when he comes out? | A crown | Motoring goggles | Leather gaiters | An enormous overcoat
exercise: «For a jolly—er—jolly——» Why does Toad stop mid-sentence? | He sees his friends' stern, silent faces | He forgets the word he wanted | The car starts by itself | He sneezes
exercise: What does the Badger tell the chauffeur? | He won't be wanted — Toad has changed his mind | To drive the car round the back and wait there | To arrest Toad | To take the friends home
exercise: What does Toad answer to «take those ridiculous things off»? | «Shan't!» | «Certainly, dear Badger, at once» | «Help, Ratty, help!» | «Why should I, Badger?»
exercise: How do they get the clothes off Toad? | The Rat sits on him and the Mole takes them off | The Badger pulls them off with one great tug | Toad takes them off himself, sobbing | The chauffeur helps
exercise: What happens to Toad's «blustering spirit» without his motor-clothes? | Much of it disappears; he giggles feebly | He gets angrier and starts shouting again | He falls asleep | He runs off
exercise: What does the book call Toad in his motor-clothes? | The Terror of the Highway | The King of the Road | Mr. Poop-Poop | The Red Toad
exercise: Whose money is Toad wasting, says the Badger? | The money his father left him | The money the Rat lent him last summer | Money borrowed from the Badger | Money the bank lent him for cars
exercise: How is Toad giving the animals «a bad name»? | Furious driving, smashes and rows with the police | Singing loudly at night, and waking up the whole river | Bad poems | Rudeness to rabbits
exercise: «Independence is all very well, but…» What comes next? | Friends won't let you make a fool of yourself beyond a limit | Everyone must do exactly what the Badger says | Toad must leave the district | Cars are fine
exercise: Where does the Badger take Toad to talk? | The smoking-room | The boat-house, down by the creek | The stable-yard | The garden
exercise: What will Toad «hear» there? | Some facts about himself | A long story about the old city | Music to cheer him up a bit | His father's will and money
test: Sam: «Badger rolls up at Toad's on his own, no cap.» (no cap = honestly) Put it right. | He brings the Rat and the Mole, walking in single file | Right: he goes alone and tells the others to stay home | He goes with the Otter | He sends a letter instead
test: Is this TRUE or FALSE? «Toad hands over his motor-clothes politely.» | False: the Rat sits on him and the Mole pulls them off | True: he folds them up neatly and hands them to the Mole with a bow | True, he's ashamed | False: he keeps them on all day
test: Ruby, lowkey shook: «why's Badger so bossy with the chauffeur, that's rude.» Why does Badger send him away so firmly? | So Toad can't drive off in the new car | Because he hates all chauffeurs and thinks they are very rude | Because the chauffeur is a weasel | Because it's raining
test: «A (comparatively) good-looking Toad.» Why the brackets around «comparatively»? | A joke — Toad is only good-looking compared with how he looks in goggles | The printer made a mistake and the brackets should not be there | Toad is the most handsome animal | It means very ugly
test: «He'll be the most converted Toad that ever was!» What does «converted» mean here? | Changed in his mind completely | Turned into a frog by some kind of magic | Moved to a new house | Made rich
test: Looking back: in chapter IV, what did the Badger promise in winter? | When the year turned, they'd take Toad in hand and make him sensible | He would buy Toad a safer, slower motor-car with his own money | He'd never speak to Toad again | He'd send Toad to the city
test: Looking back: in chapter III, what did the Rat say about calling on the Badger? | He'd be sure to be offended; you take him as and when you find him | Call any time you like — he loves visitors and parties | He is always at Toad Hall | He lives by the river
test: Looking back: in chapter II, what did the first motor-car do? | Swept past with «Poop-poop!» and the caravan crashed into the ditch | Stopped politely and gave them all a lift home in the back seat | Ran over the Rat's foot | Crashed into a tree
test: Looking back: what did Toad do the day after the caravan crash, at the end of chapter II? | Went up to Town and ordered an expensive motor-car | Went fishing with the Otter all day and forgot about cars | Mended the cart | Stayed in bed for a week
test: Looking back: in chapter II, what did the notice by Toad's creek say? | «Private. No landing allowed» | «Welcome, all friends, please land here» | «Beware of the Toad» | «Boats for hire»
test: Looking back: in chapter IV, how many smashes had Toad had, by the Rat's count? | Seven | Two | Twenty | None
test: Looking back: in chapter II, Toad said his motto in life was what? | «Live for others!» | «Speed is everything!» | «Never give up the fight» | «Toad first, always»
test: Megan from Swansea: «Badger's tamping, he's going to thump Toad.» (tamping = furious) Put it right. | He's stern, but he says he doesn't want to be too hard, and will try talking | Right: he gives Toad a beating in the stable-yard with his heavy stick, then leaves | He laughs | He calls the police
test: What would happen if the Badger had come one hour later? | Toad might already have roared off in the new car | Nothing at all would change — the car wasn't really coming | Toad would be asleep | The Rat would go alone
test: ★★★ «Whose hour, you should rather say.» Why does the Badger correct «What hour»? | He means an hour belonging to Toad, not an hour on the clock | The clock on the mantelpiece is slow and he wants it put right | He wants the time | He is hard of hearing
test: ★★★ Count the animals that go into Toad Hall in this part (count Toad too, but not the chauffeur). | 4 | 3 | 5 | 2
test: ★★★ In chapter II the cart was canary-yellow. Now the car is «bright red (Toad's favourite colour)». What do these colours show about Toad? | He loves bright, showy, look-at-me things | He likes quiet, sensible colours like grey and brown | He can't see colours | He paints everything himself
exam: Identify the principal purpose of the Badger's visit to the residence of the Rat. | The fulfilment of his undertaking to take Toad in hand | A social call of a customary and recurring nature, made each spring | The purchase of a boat | A complaint concerning the river
exam: Ascertain the arrangement under which the new motor-car was to be delivered. | On approval or return | Outright purchase, payment having already been made in full | Hire for a day | As a gift
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the measures taken to remove Toad's motoring attire? | Forcible removal by the Mole while the Rat sat upon him | Voluntary surrender of the garments by Toad, accompanied by an apology | Removal by the chauffeur | None were taken
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the Badger's charges against Toad. | Disregard of warnings, waste of his inheritance, and disrepute brought on the animals | Theft of a motor-car, driving to the public danger, and impertinence to the rural police | Neglect of his boats | Discourtesy to the Badger
